  "query_preview": "/Thursday through Sunday/... \nBy the latter half of the work week, the influence of the weakening \nsouthwestern low will all but disappear. The focus will then shift \ntoward the interplay between large scale troughing over the eastern \nPacific/West Coast, and the sub-tropical high over the Southern \nPlains and Northern Mexico. Global ensembles favor the retrograding \nback toward the Desert Southwest as we approach the weekend, likely \nstaving off any advance, at least temporarily, of more anti-cyclonic \nwaves. This progression would not completely scour residual moisture \nimmediately, so rain chances will likely linger into Thursday. \nCurrent PoPs show 30-50% chances for foothill and mountain areas and \n10-30% probabilities for the South-Central Arizona deserts. Beyond \nthat, persistent southwesterly flow would eventually dry us out \nenough to limit rain chances heading into the weekend. Another \ninflux of moisture by the end of the seven day period may bring \nrain chances back once again, but max chances at this time only \nrange between 20-30%. \n  \nEven with the competing airmasses, regional temperatures will only \nfluctuate within a few degrees through the weekend. While the ridge \nkeeps another shortwave from progressing toward the region, as \nmentioned previously, in return, the large scale troughing to our \nwest will keep the high from becoming too amplified, thankfully, \npreventing any further discussion of abnormal heat, at least during \nthis forecast period. Temperatures for the lower deserts Thursday \nthrough Sunday will range generally between 94-106 degrees, while \novernight lows hover near 70-82 degrees.",
